## Work Completed

* Continued Phase 4 by threat-modeling the SOC telemetry pipeline and defining required governance controls (cloud-agnostic)
* Authored `controls-and-threat-model.md` to secure the telemetry → detection → investigation system end-to-end
* Defined SOC pipeline security objectives:

  * integrity (evidence trustworthiness)
  * availability (burst + adversarial resilience)
  * confidentiality (sensitive log access)
  * auditability (traceability of actions and changes)
* Identified core adversarial threats against detection pipelines:

  * telemetry suppression (SOC blinding)
  * telemetry tampering (evidence manipulation)
  * baseline poisoning (slow drift)
  * alert flooding (SOC DoS)
  * rule store privilege abuse (detection bypass)
  * log access abuse (data exfiltration)
* Designed stage-wise controls across the pipeline:

  * source/agent integrity and heartbeat monitoring
  * authenticated transport and replay resistance
  * ingestion backpressure + dead-letter quarantine
  * schema validation + drift detection
  * hot/cold storage protection with immutability (WORM) and retention governance
  * bounded baseline refresh and enrichment integrity validation
  * rule governance with approvals, versioning, rollback, and audit trail
* Defined SOC governance model and access controls:

  * role separation (SOC analyst vs detection engineer vs ingestion operator vs admin)
  * least privilege + MFA + session controls
  * change approval workflows for high-impact detection modifications
* Added “monitoring the monitoring system” layer:

  * alerts for missing telemetry, lag spikes, drift anomalies, rule changes, alert storms, and unusual query behavior

